# Keep the build context small and the image clean. Without this every `docker
# build` ships the whole working tree to the daemon (~550MB here, most of it
# node_modules and .git) and `COPY . .` lands it in the builder stage.
#
# Three reasons this matters:
#  - speed: the context transfer dominates an otherwise cached build.
#  - correctness: a local node_modules would overwrite the one `npm ci` just
#    installed — host-platform binaries inside a linux/alpine image.
#  - secrets: .env holds live credentials and must never reach an image layer.

# Dependencies + build output (all reproduced inside the image)
node_modules
.nuxt
.output
dist
coverage

# Secrets and local config
.env
.env.*

# VCS and CI (not needed to build the app)
.git
.gitignore
.github
.githooks

# Editor / tooling / docs
.claude
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
*.log
CLAUDE.md
README.md
.mcp.json
start-claude.sh

# Tests and their config — the image runs the built server, nothing more
tests
vitest.config.js

# Deployment/infra assets consumed by the workflow on the host, not the image
docker
directus
directus-config
ecosystem.config.cjs
